@@ -0,0 +1,188 @@
+# Deploying the Tip Jar
+
+The site ships as a **static export on IPFS**: no server, no Vercel, no memory limits. This file is
+the exact command list. Nothing in this repo has been uploaded or deployed anywhere yet.
+
+Two things are deployed independently:
+
+| What | Where | Command |
+|------|-------|---------|
+| `TipJar.sol` | Base mainnet | `yarn deploy --network base` |
+| The frontend | IPFS (+ optional ENS) | `yarn ipfs:build` then `yarn bgipfs upload out` |
+
+The frontend build reads the contract address from `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`,
+so **deploy the contract first** — otherwise the production build ships a site whose jar address
+does not exist on Base.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Pre-flight
+
+```bash
+yarn test                       # unit tests
+FORK_TESTS=true forge test --match-contract TipJarBaseFork    # from packages/foundry
+yarn lint
+yarn next:check-types
+```
+
+Check the production values that are easy to ship wrong:
+
+- `packages/nextjs/public/thumbnail.png` is the real 1200×630 OG image, not the stock SE-2 one.
+- `packages/nextjs/.env.local` sets `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL` to a **private** Base endpoint.
+  Without it the site falls back to viem's `https://mainnet.base.org`, which rate-limits hard.
+  The build prints a warning if it is missing.
+- `scaffold.config.ts` has `pollingInterval: 3000` (Base blocks are 2s).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+```bash
+yarn account:import             # or: yarn generate — a keystore for the deployer
+yarn account                    # check it holds ETH on Base
+
+yarn deploy --network base
+yarn verify --network base
+```
+
+`yarn deploy` regenerates `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` with an `8453` entry.
+Confirm it landed before building the frontend:
+
+```bash
+grep -A2 '"8453"' packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts | head
+cast call <tipjar-address> "token()(address)" --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org
+# must print 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+```
+
+---
+
+## 2. Build the static export
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://tipjar.yourname.eth.link" yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+That one script does everything the export needs; it is
+`packages/nextjs/package.json → build:ipfs`:
+
+```bash
+node scripts/check-ipfs-build-env.mjs \
+  && rm -rf .next out \
+  && NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true \
+     NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base \
+     NODE_OPTIONS="--require ./polyfill-localstorage.cjs" \
+     yarn build
+```
+
+Why each piece is there:
+
+| Piece | Without it |
+|-------|-----------|
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` | `og:image` points at `http://localhost:3000` in every unfurl. The preflight script fails the build rather than let this ship. |
+| `rm -rf .next out` | You upload the previous build. This is the single most common IPFS mistake. |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true` | `next.config.ts` skips `output: "export"` + `trailingSlash: true`, and every route except `/` 404s on a gateway. |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base` | The shipped site points at `http://127.0.0.1:8545`. |
+| `NODE_OPTIONS=--require …` | Node 25 crashes prerendering with `localStorage.getItem is not a function`. The shim must reach the build workers, so it has to travel in `NODE_OPTIONS` — `next.config.ts` and `instrumentation.ts` run in the wrong process. |
+
+> Stop `yarn start` first. The build deletes `.next`, which a running dev server is using.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Verify the build *before* uploading
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+ls out/index.html out/debug/index.html out/blockexplorer/index.html   # every route is a directory
+grep -o '<meta property="og:image"[^>]*>' out/index.html              # absolute https URL, not localhost
+grep -o '<title>[^<]*</title>' out/index.html                         # "USDC Tip Jar", not "Scaffold-ETH 2"
+grep -rl "8453" out/_next/static/chunks/*.js | head -1                # Base is baked in
+grep -rl "<your-tipjar-address>" out/_next/static/chunks/*.js | head -1
+
+stat -c '%y %n' app/page.tsx out                                      # out/ must be NEWER than the source
+```
+
+Serve it exactly the way a gateway will — directories resolving to `index.html`:
+
+```bash
+(cd out && python3 -m http.server 8099)
+for p in "" "debug/" "blockexplorer/"; do
+  printf "%-16s %s\n" "/$p" "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://127.0.0.1:8099/$p)"
+done
+# expect 200 200 200
+```
+
+Click through it once at <http://127.0.0.1:8099> with a wallet on Base: stats load, the feed loads,
+approve → tip works, and addresses render as ENS/blockies rather than raw hex.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Upload
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+yarn bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com   # first time only
+yarn bgipfs upload out
+```
+
+Save the CID. `yarn ipfs` (from the repo root) runs the build and this upload in one step and prints
+the gateway URL.
+
+**Read the CID as evidence.** It is a hash of the bytes you uploaded, so if it is unchanged after a
+code change, those bytes were identical. It does not say which step failed — the `grep` in step 3
+tells you whether the build picked the change up, and which directory you handed the uploader tells
+you the rest.
+
+```bash
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/<CID>/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/<CID>/debug/"
+curl -s -L "https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/<CID>/" | grep 'og:image'
+```
+
+Share `https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/<CID>` for review, and **wait for approval before touching
+ENS** — the content hash is a mainnet transaction.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Point an ENS subdomain at it (optional)
+
+Two mainnet transactions, both from <https://app.ens.domains>:
+
+1. **New app only** — `yourname.eth` → *Subnames* → *New subname* → label (`tipjar`) → Next → skip
+   profile → confirm in wallet.
+2. **Every deploy** — `tipjar.yourname.eth` → *Records* → *Edit Records* → *Other* → set the
+   **Content Hash** field to `ipfs://<CID>` → save → confirm.
+
+Updating an existing app is step 2 only.
+
+Verify onchain, then through the gateway (allow 5–15 minutes for cache):
+
+```bash
+NAME=tipjar.yourname.eth
+RESOLVER=$(cast call 0x00000000000C2e074eC69A0dFb2997BA6C7d2e1e \
+  "resolver(bytes32)(address)" $(cast namehash $NAME) --rpc-url https://eth.llamarpc.com)
+cast call $RESOLVER "contenthash(bytes32)(bytes)" $(cast namehash $NAME) \
+  --rpc-url https://eth.llamarpc.com
+
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$NAME.link"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$NAME.link/debug/"
+curl -s -L "https://$NAME.link" | grep 'og:image'
+```
+
+Use `.eth.link` rather than `.eth.limo` — it behaves better on mobile. The domain you use here must
+match the `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` you built with, or the OG image will 404.
+
+---
+
+## Known limitations of the static build
+
+- **The tip feed reads logs directly.** `useScaffoldEventHistory` calls `eth_getLogs` from the
+  browser, which is fine on a fork and acceptable for a low-traffic jar, but it will hammer your RPC
+  as the history grows. Move the feed to an indexer (`.agents/skills/ponder`, `.agents/skills/subgraph`)
+  before it gets busy.
+- **`/blockexplorer` only works for the pre-rendered zero address.** Its `[address]` and `[txHash]`
+  routes need a server, so on IPFS every real address 404s. It is a local dev tool. To drop it from
+  the export, rename `packages/nextjs/app/blockexplorer` to `app/_blockexplorer-disabled` and remove
+  the footer link.
+- **Contract upgrades are new deployments.** `TipJar` has no proxy: redeploying gives a new address,
+  a new jar, and an empty feed.
